format session-log-v1
stamp 20260820.032458
editor Claude Code
model claude-opus-5
voice Kyri
host Vultr SEA NixOS VPS ai-jail pier (Daylight DC-1 hand) -- Eastern (EDT)
title Caravan mend -- a plan reported short is run again for exactly what it lost
prompt Continue the loop: with the reds queue clear, take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first, crux-first.
think The lap before this one closed REDS %96 and %97, so the queue was clear and the thread pointed where the reckon rung's own recommend had left it: a run whose plan reports short has learned something no rung yet acts on.
think Named the hole precisely before writing anything. reckon.rye derives a verdict, writes it to the wire, reads it back, and returns. A supervisor that reads its own short verdict and does nothing has learned the truth and changed nothing by learning it -- the plan is still short, and now it is short on the record. Every rung of this arc so far made a supervisor more honest; this is the first that makes one more useful.
think Chose the measurement to be work still standing when the supervisor exits, and checked it was genuinely a new number rather than a rename of the one below. It is: an overclaim is a sentence a supervisor should not have written, repaired by saying less, while unserved work is an arc nobody carried, repaired only by doing more. Same plan, different kind of wrong.
think The first real design question was where a mend learns WHERE the plan fell short. The verdict says only that it did, and the table that knows which phase is gone the moment the run returns. So the run writes a loss note beside the verdict -- one glyph per phase, x for an absorbed arc nobody served -- and the mend reads its own published verdict back before it decides anything. A supervisor that repairs from memory is repairing a plan it believes in rather than the one it published.
think Chose one glyph per phase over a packed mask deliberately, even though the record fits inside a u32 and the arc already closes turns that way. The reader here is a person at a terminal after the supervisor has exited: `.x..` says which phase of four was lost at a glance. The bound is the record's own, so it never outgrows max_queue_len.
think The second question was what a re-seated phase should carry. A phase the plan absorbed was written to ride a conferral into a dependent already standing, and that dependent is gone. So the mend re-seats the WORK and sheds the ARRANGEMENT: same domain, verb, arcs, and declared outcome, with turn renumbered and follows, follows_turns, and reserve all set aside -- because the order a phase named was an order among siblings that have already been taken up.
think This turned out to be the finding's quiet centre. Standing on its own line with no conferral to ride in on, client_b peeks menu under the read grant the document has carried all along and answers exactly what its author declared. The work the plan lost was work the document always permitted; only the dependent it was arranged through had fallen. A repair does not need new rights -- it needs a new carrier.
think Bounded the repair by the loss and made that a refusal rather than a habit. A repair that could grow into a re-run would quietly redo work the plan actually carried, which is worse than leaving it short, because it is short and lying. MendOvereach guards the caller; the wrote == seating invariant guards the implementation; unserved_after asserts served_again <= lost so no number is ever computed from an impossible pair.
think Named the price rather than hiding it. A repair costs exactly the dependents its lost phases call for -- one here, never two -- and the lap asserts reaped == reported_reaped + reseated rather than reporting the extra dependent quietly beside the finding. A cost that only appears in a printout is a cost nobody checks.
think Built the file as an accretion over reckon.rye the way every rung of this arc is built, wrapping its report rather than reaching through it, and it compiled clean on the first build. The lap ran GREEN on the first run with the numbers exactly as designed, which is what a rung that is really the next one tends to do.
think Then proved six RED paths on metal rather than trusting a first green. Withheld the loss note and the plan reported short while naming no phase it lost. Made the mend seat from its own memory and it refused MendUnread. Disarmed the whole-plan refusal and a carried plan fell through to a mend that could seat nothing. Made the repair seat every phase and unserved_after refused to compute from a repair carrying more than the plan lost. Counted the loss as untouched while publishing carried and the run refused MendUnserved. Withheld the settled verdict from the wire and it refused the same way, rather than trusting a judgment it never read back.
think One refusal changed shape under the RED proofs, which is the useful part of running them. Withholding the loss note originally reached a bare assert on seating >= 1. A verdict of short naming no phase is a record a mend cannot seat from -- the same failure as reading no verdict at all -- so it wears the same name, MendUnread, rather than a second one meaning the same thing, and the fold now refuses it before any panic can.
obs The rung tells its two readings apart by what is LEFT rather than by what is SAID, and that is the step past the one below. reckon compared two sentences about one run; mend compares two endings. Both runs confer 1 arc, reclaim 1 conferral, absorb 1 phase, lose 1 arc, and report the plan short when they first weigh it -- the reckoning underneath is untouched -- while the work standing at the end falls from 1 to 0.
obs Counted the ladder rather than carrying its number forward, since a tally repeated from memory is what REDS %93 booked. Measured: 59 ladder rungs over 62 caravan/*.rye files, 56 per-rung witnesses in the choir, all GREEN in one sing from a cold tree.
obs The council rota doc this lap sat directly on the work: a complex system that works is invariably found to have grown from a simpler system that worked. This rung is that sentence in one file -- it adds one question to a supervisor that already ran, and rewrites none of the answers underneath it.
loom witness=caravan_mend_witness phases=4 dependents_reporting=3 dependents_mending=4 conferred=1 reclaimed=1 absorbed=1 abandoned=1 reseated=1 unserved_reporting=1 unserved_mending=0 red_paths_proven=6
loom witness=caravan_suite_witness rungs=56 red=0 cold_tree=1
loom ladder rungs=59 rye_files=62 per_rung_witnesses=56 asserts_in_mend=191 usize=0
file caravan/mend.rye the rung -- the loss note on the wire, the bounded repair, four refusals, the price asserted
file tools/caravan_mend_witness.rish reads both wire notes from outside the run, exactly as an operator would
file tools/caravan_suite_witness.rish mend registered in the choir, which sings 56 from a cold tree
file caravan/README.md the ladder rows for reclaim, abandon, reckon, and mend, and the Mend section
recommend keep-going the arc has answered for reach at every moment of a dependent's life and now repairs what a fall cost, so the question standing above it is the supervisor's own judgment about whether to try: a repair that fails is a second loss, and a run that mends the same arc forever is a supervisor with no way to stop -- a bounded attempt, and a plan that names when a loss is worth carrying rather than repairing, is the mirror of this rung.
